# Every tracked text file checks out with LF on every platform.
#
# Without this, a clone on Windows with core.autocrlf=true — the default that
# Git for Windows installs, and what is set globally and system-wide on the
# development machine — writes CRLF into the working tree. `gofmt -l` then names
# every .go file as unformatted, because gofmt's output is LF and it reports by
# comparison rather than by parsing.
#
# That is not a cosmetic failure. The gofmt check is the first step in the CI
# job, so the windows-latest leg of the matrix would die there on every commit
# while Linux and macOS passed. plan.md justifies three runners on the grounds
# that a single one "would leave the developer's own platform, Windows,
# unverified" — and a leg that never reaches `go vet`, `golangci-lint`, or the
# tests leaves exactly that platform unverified, while reporting a failure that
# says nothing about the code (plan.md Verification strategy and CI matrix
# rationale; T006, T052).
#
# The stored blobs are already LF, so this governs only what checkout writes.
# No renormalising commit is required.
* text=auto eol=lf
